A||N||27||PIPO||Misterious stop running||||11||
Z||000000||PIPO||06-18-2001||09:36 AM||||My 4L, 2.5, 1987, automatic, runs OK but suddenly stops when running. No fuel is sent to the injector. It has been revised in many garages but this condition continues. Now I am trying to take the control with your expert advice, fellows! <BR>Error codes are 33 and 34, related with MAP sensor. They say MAP is OK but ECM is BAD.<BR>I have many questions:<BR>1. One way to positively verify that ECM is OK.<BR>2. The same for MAP sensor.<BR>3. Your diagnostics: All your brain storming or questions will be fully appreciated.<BR>Thanks in advance.<BR>Pipo ||||reg||11
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||06-18-2001||10:20 AM||||  PIPO, First off, how did you determine that there was no fuel to the injectors? If this is infact an anomaly, it could be your fuel pump or pehaps a vapor lock in the line. More detail is needed also. At what point in driving does this occur? Is it fine while it's cool? Does it shut off only after it warms up? are there any other warning signs before it happens? If your ECM is defunct, you can get replacements and there not to expensive, same for the MAP sensor. However I don't believe that would effect fuel flow itself (I may be wrong). <BR>  Give us a detailed list of symtems and conditions under which this occurs and we can be of more assistance.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||06-18-2001||10:22 AM||||Welcome to the Forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>One way to check the ECM is to tap on the outer cover. There were issues with ECMs but I can't recall what year(s) was (were) effected.<BR>The Ogre should be by at some point and he is hip to a lot of this kind of stuff. <BR>Check out the Ogre's cave below, it's packed with info ||||reg||14
Z||000003||PIPO||06-18-2001||11:53 AM||||Thanks for hearing me.<BR>I am the desperate owner/user, not the mechanical/electrical.<BR>This problem started about a month and I am really confused with information given to me by garage people or other supposedly experts.<BR>The car has been in garages and I do not know what changes they have made.<BR>After the last reception, (they say problem was fixed, but not)I decided to study and download web info and address to you:<P>Departing from home, good start, car stopped after 1.5 miles. (cold condition?). No previous simptoms. Then <BR>I put a wire bridge in terminals I learnt before and red the following flashing codes: 12, 33, 34, 12. (May be they were in memory since I took the car home from the garage).<BR>Difficult restart, but started again after 15-20 minutes. Returned home. Same stop again after 100 feet, and then close to home.<BR>In the past, car used to stop after 5 miles. Also in jams when iddle. Some times with easy restart and some other times with difficult restart. But it always restart.<BR>Thanks again.<BR>PIPO <P><BR>||||reg||8
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||06-18-2001||02:12 PM||||Are you sure you are not getting fuel to the injectors? It sound like (if fuel is the problem) you might have a vapor lock. I believe there is a rubber fuel line off the tank, when it gets old will start to collapse under pressure. That would be the easiest fix and least expensive. At least a place to start. If it's not fuel related, then I would say ignition module, based on problem discription. However usually the car has to be warm. I suppose that it can happen sooner if it's real bad. Anyone else? This guy is far from our locality and need some good advise. He doesn't have acces to "credible" repair facilities in South America, needless to say repair facilities familiar with the Fiero. Keep us posted PIPO!!!<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||06-18-2001||02:18 PM||||  Jose, Read these previous posts. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007935.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007935.html</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011220.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011220.html</A> <P>Tha's all have for now.<P>PEACE!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Voytek||06-18-2001||02:24 PM||||Hey Pipo!<BR>I had an '85 4 cylinder.  One day the car started running just like you've described.  Make sure the symptoms I am about to describe are the same as yours:  I drove 3 or 4 blocks and it would die, restart again after 10/15 minutes, run for a bit and then die again.  When it died, it DID turn over, just would not start.  It turned out to be the chip INSIDE the distributor cap.  Up here we can get them tested at most places.  They are usually affected by heat, hence, when they become faulty, they will short out as soon as the engine reaches a certain temperature.  The only code you'll get from this is code 12 (no signal from the distributor, I believe).  I have had THE EXACT same problem with my V6.  This is a very common problem.<BR>Bam-Bam suggested the ignition module (coil).  Yes, it's another possibility, but the only way I've ever seen them die is PERMANENTLY, i.e. you probably would not be able to drive again (the car may start again for a brief period, but that would be the end of it).<BR>As far as your other codes, they may be leftover codes from before, or you may have other faulty components (that may still permit your car to run).<BR>Let us know how it turns out!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||cscougar||06-18-2001||02:54 PM||||My 85 GT was dying mysteriously like that.  Replaced the ignition module and no more problem.  Sometimes the ignition module doesn't die all the way and can cause numerous problems.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||PIPO||06-18-2001||03:25 PM||||What Bam Bam has said is true. There should be less than ten of these cars here in Chile.<BR>I need to read these advices with somebody else in order to understand it.<BR>The most important thing by now is that I have found you guys!!!.<BR>Will contact you soon.<BR>Thanks and bye.<BR>PIPO||||reg||11
Z||000009||ROB MAC||06-18-2001||03:40 PM||||Remove the ignition module and have it tested. Coils usually die but modules can be an itermittant pain in my butt. I'm going to remove mine now.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||06-18-2001||07:26 PM||||Has the MAP sensor been replaced? It could be the new one is bad. <P>It's also posible the MAP is connected to the wrong outlet on the TBI. The correct port is shown on the label in the desk lid. If the label is missing, then plug that line and connect MAP directly to the manifold with a new line.<P>The plastic line between the MAP and TBI is prone to cracking. if the line has cracked it must be replaced. You can use regualar soft vacuum line to replace the hard stuff but keep it short.<P>After that... replace the MAP and hand trace all the wires in the engine bay. make sure none have melted or something. The MAP is common to many GM cars so you should be able to get that easy.<P>The ECM is harder. You can get the ECM from other cars but you will have to put the Fiero "chip" into it. Most GM Front Wheel Drive cars with the same motor will have the correct ECM. There is a label on the ECM... make sure any used one you find has the number sequence 1227748 on the label.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fyerstarter||06-18-2001||09:33 PM||||Replace that ignition module!! If it is an intermittent problem such as this then what is to say that when you test it it will be fine, and then put it back in the car and 20 mins later go out on you. I just got through the same ordeal where I got it tested, passed, put the sucker back in and then ended up replacing everything else ignition related on the car to no avail thinking I was running on a good ignition module the whole time. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||starfighter007@msn.com||06-18-2001||10:48 PM||||Hey Pipo how easy is it to get parts for your car down there?do they havepart houses that keep parts in stock or is every thing special order.||||reg||6
