Z||000016||Tweek||06-19-2001||04:48 PM||||My thoughts on this....<P>1. When you read the codes you will always get a code 12 at the beginning and the end of the code list. That is normal and doesn't mean there is a problem, however other codes do mean something.<P>2. I had this same symptoms in my 84, turned out to ba a bad coil, but since this is a DIS motor (that is, if it is the original motor) that is completly irrelevant.<P>3. When it dies, pop the decklid, remove the air cleaner cover and have a friend with the injector, or have a friend crank it for you. You should see fuel spraying down while the motor is turning over. Im betting you will see it.<P>4. Problem is likly electrical, as that is greatly affected by heat.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fieroluke||06-20-2001||08:06 AM||||Hi!<P>The 87/88 ECM (#1227748) is the one that has problems. Tapping on the case, while recommended by GM, is not really a scientific or deterministic approach to determine if your ECM is working okay or not.<P>The problem with this specific ECM is that it uses surface mount parts and a protective coating that ages (shrinks), tearing the parts off the board. Of course this is not visible and causes hairline cracks, causing various intermittent, hard to trace problems.<P>Tapping on the case may or may not cause the intermittent, so if the ECM passes the test, that means nothing.<P>I discovered "my" intermittent not through tapping on the case, but by tracing the copper traces on the board and resoldering all parts in the signal path. Sure enough, the metallization on a capacitor had come loose, which I then had to replace. Has run fine ever since, but this is not the recommended procedure either, don't do it unless you know what you're doing!<P>Good luck!<P>-Oliver<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000018||Cozmo||06-20-2001||09:18 AM||||Hi Pipo, <BR>   Welcome!  There are some real wizards on this forum and I'm sure with their assistance you'll be running soon.<BR>   Try what Tweek says, have a friend crank the engine over while you watch the fuel injector.  This will help determine the problem.  If you see fuel, you probably have some sort of electrical problem.  If no fuel, I'd check the fuel filter. I don't know the quality of the gas you have in Chile but I think I'd check this first.<BR>Good luck and keep us posted on your progress!<BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||1
Z||000019||PIPO||06-20-2001||11:26 AM||||It is amazing! So many people from US, Canada and Germany giving me a hand! That forces my feedback. Be patient. I have no feedback for the moment. Car is at home since the last failure. My working place is far from home and repair facilities. (Yes, I also have to work. )I do not want to send my car again to stay for another 15-20 days in a repair shop and to receive it back with the same problem. I AM STUDYING THE CASE!. Some facts:<BR>One of the repair-man told me that fuel pump is OK, but he saw that the single fuel injector works fine but sometimes, for some reason, electric signal do not reach the injector.<BR>Now we are in cold weather in Chile, so it seems not to be a hot problem at least it the initial driving.<BR>Yes, now I learned my 87 is DIS type (Distributorless Injection System). Ogro site says that PIM (Primary Ignition Module)"provides ignition coil drive current and amplification for the crank..." and on DIS motors "is located sandwiched in between the coils and the aluminium mounting plate". Is this the part that you suggest it has to be tested and replaced? <BR>Also the repair-man told me that his electrician/electronic subcontractor revised all grounds and made some resolderings in the ECM system.<BR>There is plenty of standard GM and cheap spares in Chile. There also are approved GM and expensive repair shops.<BR>On april this year leaded fuel was eliminated. I had also thought of a possible residual lead contamination coming from old storage tanks in gas stations, but I have not replaced the fuel.  <BR>Thanks again.<BR>PIPO||||reg||13
Z||000020||BAM-BAM||06-20-2001||07:58 PM||||  Pipo, The PIM is what I was refering to, not the coil. That is what I would have checked and replaced. Actually I would just replace it, it cant hurt. If you do, be sure to use the heatsink cokmpound or you'll be in the same boat for sure. Let us know if you have any luck. I would also replace the rubber fuel line/s they could be restricting fuel flow under pressure.<P>Peace,  B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000021||PIPO||08-09-2001||09:54 AM||||Please don´t laugh about me. Yes I am stupid.<BR>My car is still in the same condition, after three months...<BR>I was waiting the car repaired to contact you again to tell the cause of the problem...<BR>In the past I saw your advices, took my car home, purchased a PIM (the absolute correct spare), replaced it with the help of a friend (a car repair shop owner). And problem keeps the same. Two more repair shops. I have accepted every diagnostics... Fuel pump repaired, cylinder valves rectified, etc. etc. ECM re-reverified and OK. Now they are following again wires from ECM to the sensors and to the fuel injector.<BR>Can somebody help me with a ELECTRICAL wiring diagram of cables?<BR>The "experts" continue saying that for "some strange reason" the signal that activates the fuel injector suddenly stops, or something like that.<BR>YOUR HELP URGE. THANKS. I PROMISE TO TELL YOU THE FINAL OF THIS NIGHTMARE!   ||||reg||13
